# Overview
#
# `seam` borrows the legacy-code term for a place where a test can swap
# in other behavior without an edit (`the injection seam`). The word
# rarely says which place it means, so the reader must guess. When the
# sentence means one place, the fix names the function, interface, or
# hook (`the injection seam` becomes `the Now field that tests
# replace`). When the sentence means none, the fix deletes the word. The
# rule sits at Recommended, because the literal sense of `seam` also
# matches before a word that the rule does not list.
#
# Expression
#
# `literal` lists the construction words, such as `tape`, `roof`, and
# `weld`. `main` matches `seam` or `seams` unless a word in `literal` or
# a slash follows it. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the
# guard sits glued to `seam` and reads the token right after it. A
# `seam` at the end of a line therefore still counts.
#
# False Positives
#
# The literal sense passes before a construction word (`seam tape`, `a
# standing seam roof`). It also passes before a slash (`seam/flashing
# checks`), the form that a roofing scope list takes. The literal sense
# before any other word still matches (`press the seam flat`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule did not hit any line in the measure.py corpus, so no sample
# measures its precision.
expr literal [flashing, tape, tapes, sealer, sealant, roof, roofs, roofing, weld, welds, welded, metal, allowance, ripper]
expr main [seam, seams]![(( )@literal), /]
let message "`seam`. Name the function, interface, or hook it means, or delete the word."
let description "Flags `seam` as a metaphor for a point where code joins or swaps behavior."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Apply seam tape before the second coat."
allows "The crew quoted a standing seam metal roof."
allows "The visit covers seam/flashing/penetration checks."
allows "The Now field lets a test replace the clock."
